A||||3||fierocd||need help with steering--broken bolt||||8||
Z||000000||fierocd||04-10-2001||03:53 PM||||I took the rack and pinion off my 86GT yesterday and I got three of the four bolts out fine.  When I tried the fourth,it snapped off.  Has this happened to anyone else before.  What is the easiest way to get the broken part out.  Its not out far enough to get a good grip on it and I can't fit my drill in straight to drill it out.  This probably isn't the best idea, but would it be bad to just put the rack and pinion back with the other three in and tighten them down without the fourth bolt???||||reg||8
Z||000001||Bob Englert||04-10-2001||04:19 PM||||I have had this happen to me several times.  <P>The nuts are welded to the frame.  What I did was to cut out as much of the bolt as I could with a cut off wheel in a dremel tool and use a chisel to knock the rest of the nut off the frame.  You will be left with a perfectly good hole that you can use a regular high strength nut and bolt with.<P>It is much easier to do this with the spare tire tub out of the car.  You will then have excellent access to the mounting points.  Just remember to remove the brake line clips that hold the brake line to the tub before you remove it.<P>Good luck...<P><BR>------------------<BR>Bob<BR>2 - 87 GT's<BR>85 SE 4 cyl<p>[This message has been edited by Bob Englert (edited 04-10-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||fierocd||04-10-2001||04:21 PM||||Thanks, thats actually what I was going to try next.  I just have to wait 'till I get more cut-off wheels for my dremel||||reg||1
Z||000003||DRH||04-10-2001||05:10 PM||||I'll second the idea to take the spare tire holder out. I didn't think to do this a while back when I was working on the rack and a bolt broke. <P>I am currently redoing the bushings, ball joints, wheel hubs, etc... and decided to drop the whole suspension subframe. I took the spare tire tub out in about 10 minutes... The rack is RIGHT THERE... very easy to get to...<BR>||||reg||14
